    "q-335759": {
      "dom_id": "step-q335759",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14198813/assets/22acfe14709e4992ddadf6d1d4fcd0e2a533c93573c32dd4134ec3498b0ea805.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$ for the function above.\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "As $x$ approaches $2,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $-3.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=-3.\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f(2)$ is $-2.$ All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $2,$ the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $-3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "dom_id": "freeResponseTextbox-1",
          "tag": "mathquill",
