    "q-34924": {
      "dom_id": "step-q34924",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14198813/assets/f8b33f31c4b569da24aa8235421698859e74b144e783f0fb917a5f4b3679d834.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=1$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ approaches $0,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=2.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $-1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $-2,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=0,$ and consequently \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\n \nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1,$ and consequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x).\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
